## Zonal setup

Percentiles vs the **trailing month at the same hour** (the `tomorrow_pct` summary), with my own read against the **full 104 weeks** alongside, because the two disagree in one place that matters:

| Zone | ow | fw | ol | fl | 104wk peak-hour percentile |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **96** | **100** | 64 | 48 | wind p87, load **p98** |
| 2 | **100** | **95** | 77 | 25 | wind p86, load **p98** |
| 3 | **100** | 75 | **96** | 59 | wind p66, load **p99** |
| 4 | 75 | 59 | 64 | **16** | — |
| 5 | **100** | **87** | 41 | 59 | wind p79, load p84 |
| 21 | 66 | 79 | 41 | 51 | wind **p7**, load **p17** |

Flags above ~85: wind in zones 1, 2, 3, 5. Below ~15: zone 4 off-peak load (16).

Two things the trailing-month framing hides:

- **Load is the real story, not wind.** Zones 1/2/3 sit at the 98th–99th percentile of the two-year record on peak. The trailing-month numbers (ol 64/77/96) understate this badly — the last month has been hot across the board, so tomorrow looks ordinary against it and extreme against history.
- **Zone 21 is not neutral, it is dead.** ow 66 / fw 79 against the trailing month is p7 / p17 against two years. RZ21 wind has been near-zero all month. This matters because zone 21 produced the largest lift numbers in the run (`lnftrandl-spence1` load lift 53.7, `lnsulphspr-newell2` wind lift 26.3) — **those buckets are nowhere near being triggered tomorrow.** Ignore them.

